import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "Crypto",
	description: "The Roc Solid server platform's crypto: random bytes and AES-256-GCM from the host, with the crypto package's SHA-256, HMAC, HKDF, Base64 and Hex, for tokens, signed links and sealed secrets.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "tokens", label: "Tokens, and their digests" },
		{ id: "links", label: "Signed links" },
		{ id: "sealed", label: "Sealed secrets" },
		{ id: "modules", label: "The modules" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · crypto"
				title="Secrets, and what to do with them"
			>
				With the secrets its <a href={Paths.platform_settings}>environment</a> gives it, an app signs links,
				keeps the digest of a token rather than the token, and seals what it must keep secret in the
				database. AES-GCM and random bytes are the host's; the rest of the crypto is Roc, checked against
				published test vectors, in a package (<a href={Paths.ecosystem_crypto}><code>packages/crypto</code></a>).
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				The samples are from <code>server/test/app/Samples.roc</code>, which the platform's tests compile and
				run. It names the crypto package beside the platform (<code>crypto: "../packages/crypto/main.roc"</code>)
				and imports <code>crypto.Hmac</code> and the rest from it.
			</p>

			<Heading id="tokens">Tokens, and their digests</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_tokens.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_tokens.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>Random.bytes!(count)</code> is the operating system's randomness (<code>ring</code>'s); if there is
				none, the call crashes rather than make a key of anything less. A sign-in link's token is 32 of them;
				the database keeps its SHA-256, so a copy of the table signs no one in.
			</p>

			<Heading id="links">Signed links</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_links.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_links.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				An app that signs links derives its link-signing key from its <code>APP_SECRET</code> with HKDF, for
				that use alone, then signs what the link says with HMAC-SHA256. Checking it,
				<code>Hmac.verify_sha256</code> compares in constant time, so how long it takes doesn't tell a forger how
				much of a signature was right.
			</p>

			<Heading id="sealed">Sealed secrets</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_sealed.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_sealed.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>AesGcm.seal!(key, plaintext)</code> encrypts with AES-256-GCM under a 32-byte key
				(<code>BadKey</code> otherwise) and a fresh random 12-byte nonce each time, and answers the nonce, the
				ciphertext and the tag, one after the other. That is Web Crypto's output with the nonce in front, so
				either opens what the other sealed. <code>AesGcm.open!</code> answers <code>Forged</code> for anything
				changed, or sealed under another key.
			</p>

			<Heading id="modules">The modules</Heading>
			<table>
				<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><td><code>AesGcm</code></td><td><code>seal!</code>, <code>open!</code>: AES-256-GCM, the host's</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>Random</code></td><td><code>bytes!(count)</code>, <code>seed_u64!</code>: the operating system's</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>crypto.Sha256</code></td><td><code>hash</code>: 32 bytes; <code>hex</code>: 64 hex digits</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>crypto.Hmac</code></td><td><code>sha256(key, message)</code>; <code>verify_sha256(key, message, signature)</code> and <code>equal</code>, in constant time</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>crypto.Hkdf</code></td><td><code>sha256(key, salt, info, length)</code> (RFC 5869), <code>TooLong</code> past 8160 bytes; <code>extract</code>, <code>expand</code></td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>crypto.Base64</code></td><td><code>encode</code>, <code>decode</code>; <code>url_encode</code> (unpadded), <code>url_decode</code>; <code>encode_unpadded</code>, <code>url_encode_padded</code>, <code>lines</code> (MIME's)</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>crypto.Hex</code></td><td><code>encode</code>, <code>decode</code></td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
			<p>
				<code>AesGcm</code> and <code>Random</code> are the platform's, the host's to do. The rest are
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_crypto}>the crypto package</a>'s: Roc with no effect, so they run anywhere, a page
				in the browser and <code>expect</code>s included, checked against NIST's, RFC 4231's, RFC 4648's and RFC
				5869's vectors; <code>Sha256</code> wraps Roc's builtin <code>Crypto.SHA256</code>. Decoding is strict: one
				text, one value.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
